          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have osteoarthritis in both knees.",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a long-term condition in which the cartilage that cushions a joint gradually wears down. \"Bilateral\" means it affects both knees. The retrieved reference describes it as manageable over long periods, and it does not inevitably lead to joint replacement (*General clinical reference, Osteoarthritis*). Known contributors include age, past joint injury, excess body weight, muscle weakness, and joint shape and alignment (same source).\n\n**Management:** Guidance from rheumatology and orthopedic societies places non-drug treatment first. Structured exercise is the core treatment. It combines strengthening the muscles around the knee, keeping the joint moving through its range, and aerobic activity. Weight management is also part of care where relevant (*Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management*; *General clinical reference, Osteoarthritis*). The guidance says exercise helps regardless of how severe the wear looks on imaging, and that some discomfort during tolerable exercise does not mean the joint is being damaged. Starting with supervision, at least initially, helps people progress safely (*Exercising with osteoarthritis*). Medicines are a later step in the sequence. The retrieved passages do not give details on them, so please ask your clinician about options.\n\n**Classification:** In the US coding system (ICD-10-CM), osteoarthritis sits in codes M15 to M19. These are grouped by joint, then by cause and by side of the body. Because the code records laterality, \"both knees\" is coded differently from one knee. My search did not return the exact code for bilateral knee osteoarthritis, so ask your clinician or check the current code table.\n\n**Living with it:** The retrieved material on lifestyle is limited. It covers the exercise components described above, weight, and the fact that inactivity tends to make pain, sleep and fitness worse. My lifestyle search did not complete, so I cannot give further detail on diet or sleep from the sources available.\n\nUseful questions for your clinician include which exercise program suits you, whether weight management would help, and what treatment options are right for your knees.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
